WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.820
YUFENG GUO:
Scikit-learn has long

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:04.860
been a popular
library for getting

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.220
started with machine learning.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:09.600
But if you haven't had a
chance to try it out yet,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:10.810
that's all right.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.460
Let's go check it out together.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.820
Welcome to Cloud AI
Adventures, where

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.570
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:22.150
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:25.980
I'll help you get started using
scikit-learn in a Kaggle kernel

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.800
and point you to some
resources to guide

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.050
your learning moving forward.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:34.920
Let's start with a little
dash of history for context.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:39.420
Scikit-learn was originally
called scikits.learn,

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.260
and started life as a Google
Summer of Code project

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.630
by David Cournapeau.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:52.140
The scikit part of the name came
from it being a sciPy Toolkit.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.050
And from there,
scikit-learn has steadily

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.690
gained adoption and
popularity to where

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.620
it is today, a well
documented and well loved

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:05.250
Python machine learning library.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.320
If you take a look
at scikit-learn

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.660
at scikit-learn.org--

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.070
and you definitely should--

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:14.370
you'll quickly notice that the
version number is quite low,

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.340
0.19 as of this recording.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:18.840
Don't be scared off by that.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.360
The library has been
around for quite a while,

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.600
and is very well-maintained
and quite reliable.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.730
What's really neat
about scikit-learn

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:29.160
is the rich suite of
tools for doing things

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.980
that I like to call
"around" machine learning.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.320
It includes everything
from dataset loading

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.020
and manipulation
to preprocessing

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.480
pipelines and metrics.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.880
In my opinion, the
really incredible part

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.390
about scikit-learn is
the vast collection

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:48.370
of machine learning algorithms
that are all included.

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:50.700
And you can just try
them out, most of them

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.130
with minimal code adjustments.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.400
It's truly an amazing way
to get a handle on what

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.910
different types of models do,
as well as gain some intuition

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:03.300
around how the various
parameters for a given model

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.950
perform.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:08.520
Let's take a look now at a
simple example of scikit-learn

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.480
in action on a Kaggle kernel.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.880
We have a dataset
here of zoo animals,

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.550
just 101 different animals.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.100
The classification
task is to determine

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:24.200
which of the seven different
classes these animals are in.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.110
We'll load it up using our
typical approach with pandas.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.240
Note that the class type
field is in the final column,

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:35.340
and that's the column we'll be
interested in predicting on.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.760
In the past, we shuffled
and split the data by hand,

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.430
using pandas, as
we can see here.

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:44.580
Now, we won't run that because
scikit-learn has consolidated

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.070
all these tasks
into one function,

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.200
since they're just
so commonly used,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.990
and it's called
train_test_split.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.930
It takes care of creating
training and test data

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.510
for your features and labels.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:01.890
We can see the shapes of these
are exactly as we would expect.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.620
The default is to
use 25% of the data

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:10.010
for the test and the
remaining 75% for training.

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:13.950
We'll use a Support
Vector Classifier, or SVC,

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.030
in this example.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:17.850
But you can easily swap out this
line for a different machine

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.320
learning algorithm.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.360
Next, we'll call
fit on it, which

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.450
is really the same as train.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:28.810
And then we'll call .score to
evaluate the model performance.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:32.640
Finally, we call predict
to try out a few examples.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.940
As you can see, scikit-learn
has an API that maps really,

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.990
really similarly to the
conceptual workflow,

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:42.330
making it easy to use.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.360
So that was just a
very simple, quick look

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.310
at how you can
integrate scikit-learn

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.010
into some of the existing
work that we've been doing.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:52.960
But I know that you
can do much more.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:55.440
Dig in to the tutorials
and the documentation,

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.080
and make some awesome models.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.740
Next time, we'll talk about the
other side of machine learning

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.200
with scikit-learn,
making predictions

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.810
and how to scale that up.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.920
Thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.580
And if you enjoyed
it, please like it

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.700
and subscribe to get all
the latest episodes right

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.070
when they come out.

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:19.290
And don't forget to explore the
vast world of machine learning

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:22.600
models that scikit-learn
has to offer,

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:26.010
all with a simple, clean API.

